> you with a wand of wishing and the ability to prevent yourself from
> dying.  Sort of a watered down wizard mode.

     This is a bit backwards.  Wizard mode is for debugging purposes;
explore mode--also known as discover mode--is for experimentation.  If you
try to get into wizard mode but are not allowed (wrong user-id on multi-
user systems or wrong character name on micros), you'll end up in explore
mode instead.  Specifying -X on the command line (for the systems which
have command lines :-) explicitly requests explore mode; the 'X' command
within the game allows you to switch into explore mode while already
playing a normal game, but you won't get a wand of wishing that way.

     Real wizards don't have to ask how to get into debug mode, nor ask
what it's good for. ;-)  It's not intended for supplying infinite wishes.
(It also has nothing whatever to do with playing the role of the wizard
character.)

     Both wizard mode and explore mode are configurable options; any
particular executable may not have either one or the other or both.  The
#version command will list them if they're present in the copy you're
running.



